        "evolution": "In 1827, British physicist Sir Charles Wheatstone applied 'microphone' to an acoustic mechanical hearing device designed to amplify weak sound vibrations. In 1878, inventor David Edward Hughes resurrected the term to describe his newly invented carbon transmitter, which transformed acoustic sound waves into variable electric resistance. This breakthrough paved the way for modern telephony, radio broadcasting, public address amplification, and sound recording. Over the 20th century, dynamic, condenser, and wireless ribbon microphones transformed corporate halls and broadcast studios, while the word became universally abbreviated to 'mic' in spoken English.",
