        "howNativesThink": "To native English speakers, a 'microphone' is the primary hardware symbol of public address, vocal projection, and public speaking authority. Holding or speaking into a microphone implies addressing a wider audience and commanding attention. In everyday workplace interactions, it is overwhelmingly referred to as a 'mic' (pronounced /ma\u026ak/). With the advent of video conferencing, native speakers heavily associate microphones with audio etiquette\u2014specifically muting oneself when not speaking to avoid echo, feedback, or accidental background chatter.",
        "mentalImage": "A keynote speaker in a dark business suit standing behind an illuminated wooden podium, adjusting the flexible gooseneck microphone and tapping it lightly to verify sound levels before addressing a packed convention auditorium.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with 'loudspeaker' or 'speaker' (which performs the opposite function: converting electrical signals into audible acoustic waves). Do not use 'microphone' to designate the entire sound reproduction system (use 'PA system' or 'audio-visual equipment').",
