        "howNativesThink": "To native speakers, an 'observer' carries a dual connotation depending on context. In institutional and diplomatic settings, an observer has official accreditation but strictly passive status\u2014they have 'eyes and ears, but no voice and no vote' (e.g., UN observer missions, neutral election observers). In business news and media reporting, 'market observers' or 'industry observers' is the classic journalistic shorthand used by reporters to summarize prevailing Wall Street sentiment or industry analyst consensus without attributing quotes to a single firm.",
        "mentalImage": "An accredited monitor wearing a laminated identification badge seated in a designated perimeter row of an international regulatory assembly, taking meticulous notes on a tablet while delegates at the central dais debate and cast ballots.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with a casual bystander or tourist watching an accident on the street (use 'onlooker' or 'spectator'), a scientific investigator conducting experiments (use 'researcher'), or a person actively voting and debating in a session (use 'delegate', 'voting member', or 'participant').",
        "whenToUse": "Use when referring to someone granted non-participatory, official attendance at an assembly or audit (e.g., 'neutral election observer', 'observer status at the trade forum') or when quoting generalized expert commentary in business reportage (e.g., 'financial observers predict an interest rate cut')."
