        "howNativesThink": "Native English speakers instinctively picture a persistent professional carrying a press badge, digital audio recorder, and notebook, asking incisive follow-up questions at a press briefing. In business and public relations, a \"reporter\" represents both a crucial channel for public visibility and a demanding auditor of organizational truth. Executives treat reporters with careful deference, knowing that an unverified claim or offhand comment can quickly turn into a reputational crisis.",
        "mentalImage": "A reporter standing in a crowded press conference holding a directional microphone labeled with a network news emblem, taking rapid shorthand notes while listening to a company spokesperson unveil an environmental sustainability initiative.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not use \"reporter\" to refer to a television studio host (use \"anchor\"), an opinion writer (use \"columnist\" or \"commentator\"), or a corporate employee submitting routine internal status memos (use \"analyst\", \"author\", or \"compiler\").",
        "whenToUse": "Use when referring to an individual whose profession is gathering news, interviewing stakeholders, investigating leads, and reporting events for media organizations (e.g., \"financial reporter\", \"investigative reporter\", \"field reporter\", \"beat reporter\")."
