        "howNativesThink": "To native English speakers, a \"review\" signifies an intentional, structured evaluation rather than a casual glance. To put something \"under review\" signals that judgment and final approval are deliberately withheld while merits, errors, and metrics are scrutinized. In corporate culture, \"review\" carries personal and professional stakes: an \"annual review\" dictates raises, promotions, or performance plans, while a \"document review\" means line-by-line editorial scrutiny.",
        "mentalImage": "A department director and a senior analyst seated in a modern glass conference room, with a printed quarterly sales report and tablet open between them, systematically marking sections highlighted in amber with sticky notes and margin comments.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with \"revision\" (which denotes the actual act of altering, editing, or rewriting, whereas \"review\" is the evaluative examination preceding revisions). Do not use \"review\" for a brief, casual skim (\"glance\", \"skim\").",
